<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christianity</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Christian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christianity is a monotheistic religion based on the life and teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus of Nazareth</a>, which arose in the first century CE. It became the state religion of the Roman and Byzantine empires and of the Aksumite and Ethiopian empires, and was spread by the Carolingian, Spanish and Portuguese empires. It was also a significant minority faith under the Sasanian, Umayyad and Ottoman empires, and adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo Empire</a>'s rulers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Saturn, the Roman form of the Punic Baal Hammon, was the leading god of the countryside before <b>Christianity</b> spread rapidly in the second and third centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Aksumite society comprised a royal elite, a clergy that gained greatly in prestige and power after the adoption of <b>Christianity</b> under King Ezana in the fourth century, a merchant community of mixed origins, and a large agricultural population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Caucasus the Alans controlled the Darial Pass and held the balance between Byzantium, Persia, the Khazars and later the Arabs, and their successor, the Kingdom of Alania, adopted <b>Christianity</b> in the early tenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Christianity</b> spread only slowly, after they came under Frankish rule in the 6th and 7th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Alodia carried <b>Christianity</b> and Nubian rule to the far south of Nubia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Armenians trace their state to Urartu, founded in 860 BCE, and to the Kingdom of Armenia that in 301 CE became the first state to adopt <b>Christianity</b> as its official religion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
